From d08010ea3dab11b5500e231adee0cc3e6b89bedf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FydeOS <yang@fydeos.io>
Date: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 16:22:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/75] Skip size-compat sandboxing on ARC container so
 unresizable apps fill screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Apps that declare android:resizeableActivity="false" together with a fixed
orientation or aspect ratio (e.g. com.netease.cloudmusic with
maxAspectRatio=3.0) get put into ActivityRecord size-compat mode by AOSP,
which sandboxes them into a phone-sized rectangle (e.g. 840x1073) centered
on the tablet's full display, with a letterbox around them.

ARC++ on Chrome OS has the same upstream behavior, but for fydroid-rt we
prefer to give every standard activity the full display bounds — Chrome OS
gives every Android task the full screen anyway, and visually a letterboxed
phone window inside a 1600x2560 portrait tablet looks broken to the user.
The activity's declared maxAspectRatio still applies via applyAspectRatio()
when it would actually be exceeded; for the common case (1.6:1 tablet vs
3.0 maxAspectRatio) the limit just doesn't trigger.

Skip size-compat for standard activities when ro.boot.container=1.

Change-Id: Id961b11e728e3499fc935e6a6844fd14d8022082
---
 .../core/java/com/android/server/wm/ActivityRecord.java   | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/ActivityRecord.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/ActivityRecord.java
index d872ada1fd0c..6325a5a05dd5 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/ActivityRecord.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/ActivityRecord.java
@@ -8020,6 +8020,14 @@ final class ActivityRecord extends WindowToken implements WindowManagerService.A
             default:
                 // Fall through
         }
+        // ARC container: skip size-compat sandboxing so unresizable apps fill
+        // the (typically larger) tablet display instead of being letterboxed
+        // into a phone-sized rect. Chrome OS gives every Android task the
+        // full display bounds; the app's declared maxAspectRatio still gates
+        // applyAspectRatio() if the bounds exceed it.
+        if (Build.IS_CONTAINER && isActivityTypeStandard()) {
+            return false;
+        }
         if (inMultiWindowMode() || getWindowConfiguration().hasWindowDecorCaption()) {
             final ActivityRecord root = task != null ? task.getRootActivity() : null;
             if (root != null && root != this && !root.shouldCreateCompatDisplayInsets()) {
